# acc.add(x) of Accumulator<T, 2> (milestone2.md, "The accumulator"): the running sum s plus x, as
# add_2_2, and the running sum a of the magnitudes of the leading limbs of what was added, plus
# |x0| rounded to nearest. The limbs of x are values of the program: they pass through guard first
# (rule R3), as in sum_entry_step_2; s and a are limbs of the accumulator.
#
# The claims proposed for it in gen/kernels, which accumulator.py checks:
#   value:         s+x
#   requires:      normalized s
#   requires:      normalized x
#   requires:      nonneg a
#   ensures:       normalized t
#   ensures:       error t R c=3/(1-4*u) k=2 t=0
#   ensures:       rounded b nearest value=a+abs(x[0])
#   (needs rounded ... value=, which the generator does not have: see accumulator.py)
kernel:        acc_add_2
input:         s = s0 s1
input:         stored a
input:         supplied x = x0 x1
output:        t = t0 t1
output:        b
min_precision: 3
source:        add_2_2 on a guarded element, and the sum of the magnitudes of the leading limbs (milestone2.md); JMP17 Alg. 6, bound from MR22
license:       algorithm: published; proof: MIT
status:        unverified
entry:         yes
cone_exempt:   b, the sum of magnitudes, does not reach t0; it overflows (to +inf, its operands being nonnegative) only when a + |x0| rounds above the largest number of T, and a radius computed from it is then +inf, the whole line

g0       = guard(x0)
g1       = guard(x1)
(t0, t1) = add_2_2(s0, s1, g0, g1)
ag       = abs(g0)
b        = add(a, ag)
